    "title": "Mod-team transparency note: this month, in numbers",
    "body": "One of our commitments when this community opened was that moderation would never be a black box. So here is what the mod team actually saw this month — aggregate only, no names, ever. Reports handled: 5. Outcomes: 2 upheld with action (one post removed for financial spam, with a formal warning; one 30-day suspension for DM harassment that continued after a clear request to stop). 1 dismissed — because civil, pointed criticism of a project is not harassment, and we will keep defending that difference. 2 still in progress (one in review with a moderator assigned, one newly filed). One appeal was filed; under our recusal rule it was reviewed by a different moderator than the one who acted, and the decision was upheld. Median time from report to first moderator action: under a day. What this means for you: reporting works, reports are read by humans, and disagreeing loudly is not a reportable offence — targeting someone after they ask you to stop is. Report first, argue later; we really do read everything. Questions about the process are welcome below. Questions about specific cases will get the same answer as always: we do not discuss individuals — including with the individuals' rivals.",
